                {
                  "id": "h-weight-revenue",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Weight, Dimensions, and Revenue",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "weight-revenue"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-weight-revenue-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "It's easy to focus only on length, but dry van trailer dimensions also tie into weight ratings and profitability. The average 53-foot dry van trailer typically has a GVWR between 65,000 and 68,000 pounds. However, the trailer's weight plays a major role in how much freight you can legally carry.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-weight-revenue-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "<strong>A lighter trailer means:</strong>",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-weight-revenue-3",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "More payload capacity"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Better fuel efficiency"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Improved trailer ROI"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-weight-revenue-4",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "In such a competitive industry, even small gains in payload efficiency can add up significantly over time. For an owner-operator focused on steady, dependable growth, those details matter.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-weight-revenue-5",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Real-World Scenario: Why Dimensions Matter to Your Bottom Line",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-weight-revenue-6",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Let's say you're consistently hauling palletized consumer goods. If your trailer can reliably carry 26 pallets instead of 24, that difference compounds over hundreds of loads per year. That's more revenue without adding another truck or driver. On the other hand, if your freight is dense and weight-sensitive, interior space might not be your biggest concern. Instead, you may prioritize a lighter trailer build to maximize allowable weight. This is where understanding dry van trailer dimensions becomes strategic, not just technical.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Before deciding, ask yourself: What freight do I haul most often? Are my loads cube-heavy or weight-heavy? Do my customers require specific trailer lengths? Am I planning to scale in the next 3–5 years?",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "choosing"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-choosing-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "At Sun State Trailers, we don't rush that conversation. We take the time to understand how your operation runs so we can recommend the right trailer size for today and tomorrow. We offer dependable new, used, and rental solutions to support different stages of business growth.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "links-choosing-3-row",
                  "type": "row",
                  "props": {
                    "gap": 0
                  },
